<!-- ============================================================
     KNX Association KNX Protocol Connection Authorization Bypass
     CVE-2023-4346 (CWE-1391: Use of Weak Credentials
                  / CWE-284: Improper Access Control)
     Affected: KNX devices using Connection Authorization Option 1
               (BCU key feature) across all KNX IP-enabled hardware
     MITRE ATT&CK:
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1499.002 - Endpoint Denial of Service: Service Exhaustion Flood (TA0040 Impact)
       T1046  - Network Service Discovery (TA0007 Discovery)
       T1078  - Valid Accounts (TA0005 Privilege Escalation / TA0001 Initial Access)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-15, Federal Deadline 2026-07-29
     CISA ICS Advisory: ICSA-23-236-01
     Rule IDs: 103972-103978
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-15

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2023-4346 is a CVSS 7.5 (High) authorization bypass vulnerability
     in the KNX Association's KNX smart building automation protocol. KNX
     (formerly known as EIB, European Installation Bus) is the dominant
     open standard for building automation: it controls lighting, HVAC,
     access control, blinds, fire suppression, and other physical systems
     in commercial and residential buildings worldwide.

     The vulnerability exists in the Connection Authorization Option 1
     (CAO1) feature, which uses a BCU (Bus Coupling Unit) key to restrict
     programming access to KNX devices. Two attack surfaces:

     1. Devices without a BCU key set (the factory default):
        An attacker with network access to the KNXnet/IP layer (port 3671
        UDP/TCP) can enumerate all KNX devices on the bus, connect
        unauthenticated via KNXnet/IP tunneling, and use the ETS (Engineering
        Tool Software) programming protocol to write a BCU key of their choice
        to every device. Once set, the attacker controls the only known key,
        permanently locking the building operator out of their own automation
        system.

     2. Devices with BCU key set (intended protection):
        The BCU key in Option 1 is a 4-byte value, which can be brute-forced
        or guessed via automated tooling, allowing an attacker to gain
        programming access even when the operator believed the device was
        protected.

     The "overly restrictive" classification reflects that once a BCU key is
     set (by the attacker), the device cannot be reset without that key,
     meaning even the legitimate building operator is permanently locked out.
     CISA ICS advisory ICSA-23-236-01 documents active exploitation against
     building automation infrastructure. Limes Security coined the attack
     campaign "KNXlock" to describe systematic exploitation of this technique.

     Attack Chain:

       1. Discovery: Attacker sends KNXnet/IP SEARCH_REQUEST to the KNX IP
          multicast group (224.0.23.12:3671) or scans port 3671 directly to
          enumerate KNX IP Routers and IP Interfaces.
       2. Connect: Attacker issues a KNXnet/IP CONNECT_REQUEST to the
          identified interface (no authentication required under Option 1
          without a BCU key, or via brute-force with a weak BCU key).
       3. Program: Using ETS tunneling, attacker sends cEMI A_Memory_Write
          telegrams to the BCU key memory address of each device on the bus.
       4. Lock: BCU key is written; attacker now holds the only known
          credential. Building operator cannot access or reset the devices.
       5. Impact: Physical consequences include loss of control over lighting,
          HVAC, access control systems, fire suppression, and other critical
          building systems.

     Detection Approach:

     Layer 1 - Firewall log (rules 103972-103974):
       Rule 103972 (level 7): Base anchor - any network connection to or from
         port 3671 seen in firewall logs. KNXnet/IP exclusively uses port 3671
         UDP and TCP. Any connection to this port from non-KNX infrastructure
         hosts warrants investigation.
       Rule 103973 (level 13): Child of 103972 - source IP is publicly routable
         (non-RFC1918). KNX is an intranet building automation protocol and
         should never be reachable from the internet. External access is an
         unambiguous exploitation signal.
       Rule 103974 (level 11): Child of 103972 - destination is the KNX IP
         multicast group 224.0.23.12. SEARCH_REQUEST packets sent to this
         multicast enumerate all KNX IP interfaces on the local network.
         This is the attacker discovery phase.

     Layer 2 - Suricata IDS (rules 103975-103976):
       Rule 103975 (level 12): Suricata ICS/KNX protocol detection - any
         Suricata ET ICS signature matching KNX/EIBnet protocol activity.
         Suricata with the ET ICS ruleset can identify KNXnet/IP SEARCH_REQUEST,
         CONNECT_REQUEST, and TUNNELING_REQUEST packets at the network layer.
       Rule 103976 (level 13): Suricata KNX exploitation-specific alert - IDS
         signatures for KNX scanning, enumeration, BCU key programming, or
         unauthorized device access patterns consistent with CVE-2023-4346
         attack tooling.

     Layer 3 - Raw log / KNX management software (rule 103977):
       Rule 103977 (level 14): Matches application-level log messages from
         KNX management software (ETS, KNX Virtual, or custom KNX syslog
         forwarders) or Zeek ICS protocol analyzer output containing KNXnet/IP
         service type names, BCU key writes, or programming mode access.
         These strings appear in vendor KNX diagnostic logs, Zeek KNX
         analyzer NOTICE logs, and some ICS security monitoring tools.

     Layer 4 - Frequency (rule 103978):
       Rule 103978 (level 9): Rapid probe burst from a single source to port
         3671 - 10+ connection attempts in 60 seconds. Automated KNX scanner
         tools (KNXmap, knxd, custom exploit scripts) issue rapid sequential
         connections when scanning for unprotected KNX interfaces.

     Log Source Prerequisites:
       Firewall layer: nftables/iptables packet filter logs forwarded to Wazuh.
         Field names: data.src_ip (source IP), data.dest_ip (destination IP),
         data.dest_port (destination port). Events arrive in the firewall group.
       Suricata layer: Suricata EVE JSON logs forwarded to Wazuh. ICS/KNX
         detection requires the Emerging Threats ICS ruleset (ET ICS category)
         or custom KNX Suricata rules. Parent SID: 86601 (Wazuh Suricata rule).
       Application/KNX layer: KNX management tool syslog, Zeek KNX protocol
         analyzer NOTICE logs, or custom KNX diagnostic logging. These produce
         plain-text log lines containing KNXnet/IP service type names.

     Severity Tiers:
        7 - Firewall: any connection to KNXnet/IP port 3671 (anchor, correlation only)
       13 - Firewall: port 3671 connection from external (non-RFC1918) source IP
       11 - Firewall: KNX multicast discovery traffic to 224.0.23.12
       12 - Suricata: any ET ICS signature matching KNX/EIBnet protocol
       13 - Suricata: KNX exploitation-specific IDS signature
       14 - Application log: KNXnet/IP CONNECT_REQUEST, BCU key write, or programming mode
        9 - Frequency: 10+ connections to port 3671 from same source in 60 seconds

     False Positive Guidance:
       103972: Legitimate KNXnet/IP traffic from authorized building management
         workstations, ETS (Engineering Tool Software) PCs, or KNX IP Router
         devices will fire this anchor. Correlate with 103973 and 103978 for
         exploitation signals; suppress for known-good KNX infrastructure IPs.
       103973: No benign business justification exists for internet-routable IPs
         connecting to port 3671. If KNX is deliberately exposed to the internet
         (strongly inadvisable), this rule would need an override for the
         specific permitted source CIDRs.
       103974: KNX IP Routers and ETS installation software legitimately send
         SEARCH_REQUEST multicast during network startup and when discovering
         KNX IP interfaces. Alert is meaningful when originating from
         unexpected hosts (e.g., workstations that are not authorized ETS PCs).
       103975: ET ICS rules may fire during normal authorized ETS commissioning
         sessions. Baseline expected commissioning windows and alert on
         deviations or unexpected source IPs.
       103976: KNX exploitation-specific signatures have very low false positive
         rates; treat any match as requiring immediate investigation.
       103977: KNX management software may log CONNECT_REQUEST messages during
         authorized device programming sessions. Correlate with time-of-day
         (outside maintenance windows) and source host (non-ETS workstations).
       103978: Automated KNX diagnostic tools run on a schedule may generate
         rapid sequential connections; baseline known-good scan sources.
         Treat unknown source IPs as confirmed scanning activity.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="impact,initial_access,ics,knx,building_automation,cisa_kev,cve_2023_4346,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: Firewall log rules (nftables/iptables)
       Rule IDs: 103972-103974

       KNXnet/IP exclusively uses port 3671 (UDP for tunneling and routing,
       TCP for secure tunneling). Any connection to or from port 3671 is
       scoped to KNX building automation traffic. All three Layer 1 rules
       detect at the packet-filter layer before any application decode.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: any connection to KNXnet/IP port 3671 seen in firewall logs.

       KNXnet/IP (KNX over IP) is the network-layer protocol for controlling
       KNX building automation devices over IP infrastructure. Port 3671 is the
       IANA-registered port exclusively used by KNXnet/IP. All traffic on this
       port is KNX-related.

       This anchor fires on any packet destined for port 3671, including both
       inbound connections from building management workstations and unexpected
       connections from unauthorized hosts. The level 7 severity is
       informational - it correlates with child rules 103973, 103974, and 103978
       to distinguish legitimate building automation traffic from CVE-2023-4346
       exploitation attempts. Suppress for well-known authorized KNX
       infrastructure source IPs. -->
  <rule id="103972" level="7">
    <if_group>firewall</if_group>
    <field name="data.dest_port">^3671$</field>
    <description>KNXnet/IP: Connection to KNX building automation port 3671 on $(data.dest_ip) from $(data.src_ip) -- KNXnet/IP (port 3671) traffic detected; investigate source against authorized KNX infrastructure; correlate with rules 103973-103978 for CVE-2023-4346 exploitation signals (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>knx,ics,building_automation,knxnet_ip,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- External source connecting to KNXnet/IP port 3671.

       KNX is a local-network building automation protocol. KNXnet/IP devices
       (IP Routers, IP Interfaces, IP Gateways) are intranet-only appliances.
       There is no legitimate business reason for a publicly routable
       (non-RFC1918) IP address to connect to port 3671 on any building
       automation device.

       This rule matches when the source IP is not in any RFC1918 private
       address space (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), loopback (127.0.0.1),
       or IPv6 link-local/loopback. Any non-RFC1918 source is an external,
       internet-routable IP - indicating that the KNX IP interface is
       internet-exposed and being accessed by an attacker.

       CVE-2023-4346 requires network-layer access to port 3671. In
       properly segmented networks, this access is limited to authorized
       building management workstations on the building automation VLAN.
       External access is an unambiguous exploitation indicator and requires
       immediate investigation: identify the source IP, capture traffic,
       audit all KNX device BCU key states, and assess whether unauthorized
       BCU key writes occurred. -->
  <rule id="103973" level="13">
    <if_sid>103972</if_sid>
    <field name="data.src_ip" negate="yes" type="pcre2">^(10\.|172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.|192\.168\.|127\.|::1$|fe80:)</field>
    <description>CVE-2023-4346 KNXnet/IP: External source $(data.src_ip) connecting to KNX port 3671 on $(data.dest_ip) -- internet-routable IP accessing building automation network; KNXnet/IP is a local-network protocol with no legitimate internet exposure; active CVE-2023-4346 exploitation likely; audit KNX device BCU key states immediately (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>knx,ics,building_automation,knxnet_ip,external_access,cve_2023_4346,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- KNX multicast discovery traffic: SEARCH_REQUEST to 224.0.23.12.

       KNXnet/IP Discovery uses the multicast group address 224.0.23.12 on
       port 3671 UDP. An ETS (Engineering Tool Software) client or KNX
       scanner sends a SEARCH_REQUEST datagram to this multicast address to
       discover all KNX IP interfaces on the local network segment. The
       interfaces respond with SEARCH_RESPONSE packets identifying themselves.

       In the CVE-2023-4346 attack chain, this is Phase 1 (Discovery):
       the attacker enumerates all available KNX IP Interfaces and IP Routers
       before initiating unauthenticated CONNECT_REQUESTs.

       224.0.23.12 is the IANA-registered multicast address reserved
       exclusively for KNXnet/IP. Traffic to this address should only
       originate from authorized ETS workstations during scheduled
       commissioning or maintenance windows. Multicast traffic from
       unexpected hosts or outside maintenance windows indicates unauthorized
       KNX device enumeration consistent with the KNXlock attack campaign
       (CVE-2023-4346). -->
  <rule id="103974" level="11">
    <if_sid>103972</if_sid>
    <field name="data.dest_ip" type="pcre2">^224\.0\.23\.12$</field>
    <description>CVE-2023-4346 KNXnet/IP: KNX discovery multicast SEARCH_REQUEST from $(data.src_ip) to 224.0.23.12:3671 -- KNXnet/IP device discovery on the KNX multicast group; attacker enumerating KNX IP interfaces for CVE-2023-4346 BCU key bypass attack; investigate source host against authorized ETS workstation list (T1046/T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1046</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>knx,ics,building_automation,knxnet_ip,discovery,multicast,cve_2023_4346,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: Suricata IDS rules
       Rule IDs: 103975-103976

       Suricata with the Emerging Threats ICS ruleset (ET ICS category)
       can decode KNXnet/IP packet headers and match on specific service
       type values in the KNXnet/IP frame. The parent SID 86601 is the
       Wazuh built-in rule for all Suricata EVE JSON alerts.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Suricata: any IDS alert matching KNX/EIBnet building automation protocol.

       The Emerging Threats ICS ruleset includes signatures for KNXnet/IP
       protocol activity, including SEARCH_REQUEST (service type 0x0201),
       CONNECT_REQUEST (0x0202), TUNNELING_REQUEST (0x0420), and other
       KNXnet/IP service types. When Suricata fires any ICS/KNX signature,
       this rule alerts for analyst review.

       EIBnet is the legacy name for the KNXnet/IP protocol; the terms are
       used interchangeably in vendor documentation and some IDS signatures
       (particularly older Snort/Suricata rules).

       This rule is broader than 103976 and covers any Suricata detection
       event related to KNX/EIBnet, even if not exploitation-specific.
       False positives are possible during authorized commissioning sessions;
       correlate with source IP and time-of-day against the maintenance
       change calendar. -->
  <rule id="103975" level="12">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(KNX|EIBnet|knxnet|knxip|KNX\/IP|building.automat)</field>
    <description>Suricata ICS: KNXnet/IP or EIBnet building automation protocol alert -- $(alert.signature) from $(alert.src_ip) to $(alert.dest_ip):$(alert.dest_port); KNX IDS signature fired; investigate source against authorized ETS workstation list for CVE-2023-4346 exploitation (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,knx,ics,building_automation,cve_2023_4346,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Suricata: KNX exploitation, enumeration, or BCU key attack signatures.

       This rule targets Suricata signatures that specifically indicate active
       exploitation of CVE-2023-4346 or the KNXlock attack pattern:
         - CONNECT_REQUEST from unauthorized source (tunnel establishment)
         - Device scanning/enumeration (rapid SEARCH_REQUEST bursts)
         - BCU key write operations (A_Memory_Write to BCU address)
         - Programming mode access without prior authentication
         - KNX group object write from unexpected source (actuator manipulation)
         - KNXmap or KNXscan tool signatures

       Unlike 103975 (any KNX IDS alert), this rule matches signatures that
       explicitly reference exploitation-related terms. These signatures have
       a very low false positive rate and should trigger immediate escalation
       to the building operations team to audit device BCU key states and
       assess whether unauthorized writes occurred. -->
  <rule id="103976" level="13">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(KNX|EIBnet|knxnet).*(exploit|scan|enum|connect.request|BCU|program|lock|group.write|memory.write|tunnel|unauthorized)</field>
    <description>Suricata ICS: KNX exploitation or enumeration alert -- $(alert.signature) from $(alert.src_ip) to $(alert.dest_ip):$(alert.dest_port); KNX-specific IDS signature indicating unauthorized access or BCU key write consistent with CVE-2023-4346 KNXlock attack; escalate to building operations immediately (T1190/T1499.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1499.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,knx,ics,building_automation,exploitation,cve_2023_4346,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 3: Application log / KNX management software (rule 103977)

       KNX management and diagnostic software (ETS, KNX Virtual, OpenKNX,
       linknx, knxd) and Zeek ICS protocol analyzers emit structured log
       messages containing KNXnet/IP service type names, BCU key operations,
       and programming mode events. This rule matches those log strings.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Raw log: KNXnet/IP programming activity, BCU key write, or CONNECT_REQUEST.

       This rule matches plain-text log lines produced by:
         - KNX management software (ETS, KNX Virtual, knxd, linknx) that
           forwards diagnostic logs to syslog or Wazuh agent file monitoring
         - Zeek (Bro) ICS KNXnet/IP protocol analyzer (zeek-knx package),
           which emits NOTICE log entries for protocol service types
         - Custom building automation monitoring systems that log KNXnet/IP
           service type names in audit trails
         - OpenHAB or Home Assistant with KNX binding verbose logging enabled

       The pattern matches strings indicating:
         - CONNECT_REQUEST: KNXnet/IP tunnel connection establishment
           (the first step in the CVE-2023-4346 exploit chain)
         - BCU_KEY or BCU key: Bus Coupling Unit key write operation
           (the payload of CVE-2023-4346; once written, device is locked)
         - A_Memory_Write: KNX application protocol memory write telegram
           (the low-level operation that sets the BCU key in device firmware)
         - programming_mode or program_mode: ETS programming mode activation
           (required to write BCU keys to KNX devices)
         - TUNNELING_REQUEST with A_GroupValue_Write: unauthorized group
           object write (attacker controlling physical building systems)

       Matching any of these terms in a log line outside of an authorized
       maintenance window is a high-confidence CVE-2023-4346 exploitation
       indicator. The BCU key write specifically represents the attacker
       locking the device; incident response must begin immediately to
       determine which devices were affected and whether the building operator
       still has a path to recovery. -->
  <rule id="103977" level="14">
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:KNXnet|EIBnet|knxd|linknx|openknx).*(?:CONNECT_REQUEST|BCU[_\s]key|BCU_KEY|A_Memory_Write|programming[_\s]mode|program[_\s]mode|A_GroupValue_Write)|(?:BCU[_\s]key|BCU_KEY).*(?:KNX|EIB|knxnet)|(?:KNX|knxnet|EIBnet).*(?:lock(?:ed|ing)?|unauthorized.*program|unauthorized.*BCU)</regex>
    <description>CVE-2023-4346 KNXnet/IP: KNX management log shows programming access, BCU key write, or CONNECT_REQUEST -- $(agent.name); unauthenticated BCU key write or programming mode access is the CVE-2023-4346 attack payload; KNX device lockout may be in progress; escalate to building operations and isolate affected KNX IP interfaces immediately (T1190/T1499.002/T1078)</description>
    <options>alert_by_email</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1499.002</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>knx,ics,building_automation,bcu_key,programming,cve_2023_4346,critical,high_confidence,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 4: Frequency-based detection (rule 103978)

       Automated KNX exploitation tools (KNXmap, knxd scan mode, custom
       Python scripts using the xknx library) issue rapid sequential
       KNXnet/IP connection attempts when scanning for unprotected KNX IP
       interfaces or enumerating KNX device addresses on the bus.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Rapid probe burst to KNXnet/IP port 3671 from a single source.

       Automated KNX attack tools issue high-frequency connection attempts
       when:
         1. Scanning for KNX IP Interfaces: rapid sequential CONNECT_REQUEST
            to discover which IP addresses on the network respond as KNX
            tunneling servers
         2. KNX individual address enumeration: scanning through all
            KNX device individual addresses (1.1.1 through 15.15.255)
            to build a map of devices on the bus before BCU key attack
         3. BCU key brute force: iterating through possible 4-byte BCU key
            values against a KNX device protected by Connection Auth Option 1

       A burst of 10 or more connections from the same source IP to port 3671
       within 60 seconds has no legitimate explanation in normal building
       operations. Authorized ETS commissioning tools connect to a specific
       known KNX IP Interface and do not generate rapid bursts. Treat any
       such burst from an unrecognized source IP as automated KNX exploitation
       tooling and investigate the source host immediately. -->
  <rule id="103978" level="9" frequency="10" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103972</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>data.src_ip</same_field>
    <description>CVE-2023-4346 KNXnet/IP: Rapid probe burst from $(data.src_ip) to KNX port 3671 -- 10+ connection attempts in 60 seconds from same source; automated KNX scanner or CVE-2023-4346 exploitation tool (KNXmap, xknx, knxd) fingerprint; investigate source host for unauthorized KNX tooling (T1046)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1046</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>knx,ics,building_automation,probe_burst,scanner,cve_2023_4346,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
